WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.668
- Happy 2019, everyone.

00:00:01.668 --> 00:00:03.343
We're all still here.

00:00:04.404 --> 00:00:07.700
Donald Trump is on the verge
of making some history.

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:08.713
Again!

00:00:08.713 --> 00:00:10.842
And like most of the
history Trump has made,

00:00:10.842 --> 00:00:13.350
this isn't really the
good kind of history.

00:00:13.350 --> 00:00:15.812
The federal government,
or at least a chunk of it,

00:00:15.812 --> 00:00:19.430
has been shut down
since December the 22nd.

00:00:19.430 --> 00:00:21.039
Now, while you probably
haven't been paying

00:00:21.039 --> 00:00:24.130
that much attention to that
fact, what with, you know,

00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:25.587
the whole Christmas and New Years thing,

00:00:25.587 --> 00:00:28.469
we are rapidly approaching the longest

00:00:28.469 --> 00:00:31.410
government shutdown in modern history.

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:34.866
Repeat, the longest government
shutdown in modern history.

00:00:34.866 --> 00:00:37.810
The current record is
21 days, which happened

00:00:37.810 --> 00:00:41.950
between December 16th,
1995, and January 6th, 1996.

00:00:41.950 --> 00:00:45.891
The second longest was 18
days, way back in 1978.

00:00:45.891 --> 00:00:47.641
And then, right there tied for second

00:00:47.641 --> 00:00:52.260
and rapidly shooting up the
charts is this here shutdown.

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:54.033
Before I get into why this shutdown

00:00:54.033 --> 00:00:56.109
will almost certainly
break all the records

00:00:56.109 --> 00:00:58.800
that have come before it,
it's probably worth explaining

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:02.600
how and why the government
actually shuts down.

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:03.507
Alright, let's do this thing.

00:01:03.507 --> 00:01:05.760
Congress is in charge of giving

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:07.310
the various departments and agencies

00:01:07.310 --> 00:01:09.250
the money they need to operate every year.

00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:10.615
It's right there in the Constitution.

00:01:10.615 --> 00:01:13.858
They do this by passing
12 appropriations bills

00:01:13.858 --> 00:01:18.250
by the end of the fiscal year,
which is September the 30th.

00:01:18.250 --> 00:01:19.270
Simple!

00:01:19.270 --> 00:01:20.103
Right?

00:01:20.103 --> 00:01:21.840
House and the Senate have an entire year

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:23.619
to pass 12 bills that keep
the government up and running.

00:01:23.619 --> 00:01:25.340
That's one bill a month.

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:27.030
That shouldn't be much of a problem.

00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:28.315
We got this, guys.

00:01:28.315 --> 00:01:31.504
Except that the two parties
have increasingly used

00:01:31.504 --> 00:01:34.608
this budgeting process and
that September 30th deadline

00:01:34.608 --> 00:01:36.577
as a negotiating tactic to get one

00:01:36.577 --> 00:01:39.026
of the two chambers of
Congress or the president

00:01:39.026 --> 00:01:41.388
to give them something they want.

00:01:41.388 --> 00:01:45.520
Take the '95-'96 shutdown,
the current record holder.

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:47.662
The issue in that one
was a major disagreement

00:01:47.662 --> 00:01:49.696
between President Bill
Clinton, a Democrat,

00:01:49.696 --> 00:01:52.137
and House Speaker Newt
Gingrich, a Republican,

00:01:52.137 --> 00:01:54.204
over how much money should be given

00:01:54.204 --> 00:01:58.280
to priorities like Medicare
and public education.

00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:00.880
Or the 2013 shutdown,
that one wound up being

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:02.643
a vehicle for conservative Republicans,

00:02:02.643 --> 00:02:05.680
led by Texas senator Ted Cruz,
you may have heard of him,

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:08.282
to force a showdown over Obamacare.

00:02:08.282 --> 00:02:11.890
Conservatives attached the
repeal of the Affordable Care Act

00:02:11.890 --> 00:02:14.309
to the legislation that
was supposed to keep

00:02:14.309 --> 00:02:15.952
the government up and running.

00:02:15.952 --> 00:02:19.038
Which amounted to pouring
bleach in the punch,

00:02:19.038 --> 00:02:22.248
do not try that at home,
and so the government,

00:02:22.248 --> 00:02:24.227
yeah, you know the story, shut down.

00:02:24.227 --> 00:02:27.197
This latest fight is similarly symbolic.

00:02:27.197 --> 00:02:28.929
Much of the government is actually funded

00:02:28.929 --> 00:02:31.730
via appropriations bills that have already

00:02:31.730 --> 00:02:33.840
passed Congress and been
signed by the president.

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:35.989
The shutdown is, therefore, only partial,

00:02:35.989 --> 00:02:39.052
affecting about 25%-ish of the government.

00:02:39.052 --> 00:02:41.900
That amounts to nine federal departments

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:43.566
and a variety of related agencies.

00:02:43.566 --> 00:02:46.369
All told, it hits 800,000 federal workers,

00:02:46.369 --> 00:02:50.955
about half of whom are working
for no pay right about now.

00:02:50.955 --> 00:02:52.931
Now, the main fight here is over funding

00:02:52.931 --> 00:02:54.701
for the Department of Homeland Security.

00:02:54.701 --> 00:02:57.899
President Trump wants
$5 billion appropriated

00:02:57.899 --> 00:03:01.260
for his much ballyhooed
wall on the Mexico border.

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:03.710
House Speaker Nancy Pelosi says, no.

00:03:03.710 --> 00:03:04.860
- We're not doing a wall.

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:08.960
Does anybody have any doubt
about, we are not doing a wall.

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:12.345
This is not a wall between
Mexico and the United States

00:03:12.345 --> 00:03:14.621
that the president is creating here.

00:03:14.621 --> 00:03:18.981
It's a wall between reality
and his constituents.

00:03:18.981 --> 00:03:20.570
- So, here we are.

00:03:20.570 --> 00:03:22.750
The immovable force and
the unstoppable object.

00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:24.988
Or, more accurately, a game of chicken

00:03:24.988 --> 00:03:27.412
with the livelihoods of
hundreds of thousands

00:03:27.412 --> 00:03:30.960
of federal workers in the balance.

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:32.965
This is, in a word, dumb.

00:03:32.965 --> 00:03:35.875
It is in two words super dumb.

00:03:35.875 --> 00:03:39.580
Even the most hard-line
anti-immigration voices

00:03:39.580 --> 00:03:41.790
don't see the construction of a wall along

00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:44.785
the southern border as a major
priority, they just don't.

00:03:44.785 --> 00:03:46.717
And many Democrats have, in the past,

00:03:46.717 --> 00:03:49.823
voted for miles of fencing along
some portions of the border

00:03:49.823 --> 00:03:52.013
between the United States and Mexico.

00:03:52.013 --> 00:03:54.655
This is, folks, a political fight.

00:03:54.655 --> 00:03:56.520
Trump thinks the wall promise

00:03:56.520 --> 00:03:59.442
is one of the key reasons
why he is president today.

00:03:59.442 --> 00:04:02.440
At virtually every rally
during the 2016 campaign,

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:06.258
Trump gloried in chants of
build that wall from the crowds.

00:04:06.258 --> 00:04:09.379
It's a symbol to him of the
anti-political correctness

00:04:09.379 --> 00:04:12.680
and speaking-truth-to-power-ness

00:04:12.680 --> 00:04:14.639
that made people vote for
him in the first place.

00:04:14.639 --> 00:04:16.178
Sidebar, the idea of a wall

00:04:16.178 --> 00:04:19.297
at the southern border
is not terribly popular.

00:04:19.297 --> 00:04:23.110
A CNN poll in December found
that 57% of the US public

00:04:23.110 --> 00:04:27.150
is against the wall, compared
with just 38% who favor it.

00:04:27.150 --> 00:04:29.690
Democrats, who have just
retaken control of the House,

00:04:29.690 --> 00:04:32.539
are emboldened by the
results of the 2018 election,

00:04:32.539 --> 00:04:34.804
and know that their base doesn't want

00:04:34.804 --> 00:04:38.022
any sort of deal-making
with Trump on any issue,

00:04:38.022 --> 00:04:40.188
but most especially the issue of the wall,

00:04:40.188 --> 00:04:43.931
which they see as the height
of the president's hubris.

00:04:43.931 --> 00:04:46.784
Wanna understand how far
apart the two sides are?

00:04:46.784 --> 00:04:48.240
Try this on for size.

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:50.486
After a meeting at the White
House just after New Years,

00:04:50.486 --> 00:04:53.046
Chuck Schumer, the top ranking
Democrat in the Senate,

00:04:53.046 --> 00:04:55.096
and Nancy Pelosi, the new House Speaker,

00:04:55.096 --> 00:04:57.759
said the negotiations with Trump had been,

00:04:57.759 --> 00:05:00.086
quote, contentious, end quote.

00:05:00.086 --> 00:05:01.821
Just a few minutes later, the president

00:05:01.821 --> 00:05:02.842
appeared in the Rose Garden

00:05:02.842 --> 00:05:06.386
and cast the same meeting
as, quote, productive.

00:05:06.386 --> 00:05:07.425
What?

00:05:07.425 --> 00:05:09.036
The two parties seem to be talking

00:05:09.036 --> 00:05:13.250
entirely past one another, two ships

00:05:13.250 --> 00:05:14.254
passing in the night.

00:05:14.254 --> 00:05:16.600
Which is why I will go out on a limb

00:05:16.600 --> 00:05:18.780
and say that this 2019 shutdown

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:22.940
is going into the record
books as the longest one ever.

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:25.490
If I'm being honest, it's
actually not that much of a limb.

00:05:25.490 --> 00:05:27.320
Even if the White House
and Democrats and Congress

00:05:27.320 --> 00:05:29.640
were to agree today on a deal,

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:30.899
and again, that ain't happening,

00:05:30.899 --> 00:05:33.900
it would take days to write
a piece of legislation

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:36.685
detailing the agreement, and
then pass it through Congress.

00:05:36.685 --> 00:05:38.217
Logistics, people!

00:05:38.217 --> 00:05:40.326
It always comes back to logistics.

00:05:40.326 --> 00:05:42.446
Modern politics and politicians

00:05:42.446 --> 00:05:45.478
do very little quickly or all that well,

00:05:45.478 --> 00:05:50.450
and that includes keeping
their own government open.

00:05:50.450 --> 00:05:53.185
And that is the point.

00:05:53.185 --> 00:05:56.672
We do this twice a week every single week!

00:05:56.672 --> 00:06:00.543
Tune in every Tuesday and
Thursday for hot fresh content.

